  {
   "id": "m21-history-science-2",
   "code": "IL-21",
   "title": "History of Science II — 1800 to the Present",
   "weight": 1,
   "summary": "Energy, evolution, electromagnetism, relativity, quantum theory, and computation: the two centuries that built the modern world.",
   "objectives": [
    "Trace the major 19th–20th century revolutions",
    "Explain the significance of Maxwell, Darwin, Einstein, and the quantum founders",
    "Describe the birth of computing",
    "Connect scientific revolutions to engineering practice"
   ],
   "notes": [
    {
     "title": "The 19th century: energy and evolution",
     "body": "**Thermodynamics** (Carnot, Clausius, Kelvin): energy conservation and entropy — the science of engines became the science of everything. **Darwin** (*Origin of Species*, 1859): natural selection explains adaptation without design. **Maxwell** (1860s): four equations unifying electricity, magnetism, and light — predicting electromagnetic waves, confirmed by Hertz (1887)."
    },
    {
     "title": "1900–1930: relativity and quantum",
     "body": "**Planck** (1900) quantized energy to solve blackbody radiation; **Einstein** (1905: photoelectric effect, special relativity; 1915: general relativity) rewrote space, time, and gravity. **Bohr, Heisenberg, Schrödinger, Dirac** (1920s) built quantum mechanics — probabilistic at its core, technologically the foundation of semiconductors and lasers."
    },
    {
     "title": "The information age",
     "body": "**Turing** (1936): the universal machine — computation's theoretical foundation, before any general-purpose computer existed. **Shannon** (1948): information theory — bits, channel capacity, the mathematics of communication. ENIAC (1945), the transistor (1947), integrated circuits (1958), ARPANET (1969): theory became infrastructure within a generation."
    },
    {
     "title": "Structure of the story",
     "body": "Notice the pattern: unification (Maxwell, Einstein), then mathematization, then instruments, then engineering. Each revolution preserved the old theory as a limiting case — Newton survives inside Einstein at low speeds. Science grows by *containing* its past, not erasing it."
    }
   ],
   "cards": [
    {
     "front": "Maxwell's achievement (1860s)?",
     "back": "Unifying electricity, magnetism, and light in four equations; predicting EM waves."
    },
    {
     "front": "Darwin 1859: the mechanism?",
     "back": "Natural selection — differential survival and reproduction of heritable variants."
    },
    {
     "front": "Einstein's 1905 'miracle year' outputs?",
     "back": "Photoelectric effect (light quanta), special relativity, Brownian motion (atoms)."
    },
    {
     "front": "Turing 1936 and Shannon 1948: contributions?",
     "back": "Turing: the universal machine (computation theory). Shannon: information theory (bits, channel capacity)."
    },
    {
     "front": "How do revolutions relate to old theories?",
     "back": "The old theory survives as a limiting case — e.g., Newton inside Einstein at low speeds."
    }
   ],
