  {
   "id": "m21-history-tech",
   "code": "IL-21",
   "title": "History of Technology",
   "weight": 1,
   "summary": "From the steam engine to the internet: how technologies diffuse, why incumbents miss transitions, and what the industrial revolutions teach builders.",
   "objectives": [
    "Name the industrial revolutions and their core technologies",
    "Explain technology diffusion and the S-curve",
    "Describe why incumbents fail at transitions",
    "Draw lessons for current platform shifts"
   ],
   "notes": [
    {
     "title": "The industrial revolutions",
     "body": "**First** (~1760–1840): steam, mechanized textiles, iron — Britain's factories. **Second** (~1870–1914): electricity, steel, chemicals, the internal-combustion engine — mass production. **Third** (~1960s–): electronics, computers, the internet — information as the factor of production. Some historians count a **fourth** (cyber-physical systems, AI) from the 2010s."
    },
    {
     "title": "Diffusion and the S-curve",
     "body": "Technologies diffuse along an **S-curve**: slow uptake, explosive growth, saturation. **Rogers'** adopter categories (innovators → laggards) and **Moore's** 'chasm' between early adopters and the early majority describe the same curve's social shape. Infrastructure (railways, grids, fiber) determines the slope — technology without distribution is a demo."
    },
    {
     "title": "Why incumbents miss it",
     "body": "**Christensen's** *Innovator's Dilemma* (1997): incumbents rationally serve their best customers with sustaining improvements — and are blindsided by **disruptive** technologies that start worse and cheaper, then climb the S-curve. Kodak invented the digital camera (1975) and still lost; the dilemma is structural, not a failure of intelligence."
    },
    {
     "title": "The internet's lesson",
     "body": "ARPANET (1969) → TCP/IP standardization (1983) → the Web (1990–) → broadband → mobile: each layer enabled the next, and open protocols beat walled gardens at the infrastructure layer. The pattern for builders: bet on open layers, expect the application layer to surprise you, and remember that distribution — not just invention — decides winners."
    }
   ],
   "cards": [
    {
     "front": "The three (or four) industrial revolutions and their cores?",
     "back": "1st: steam/textiles/iron. 2nd: electricity/steel/chemicals/engines. 3rd: electronics/computers/internet. 4th (claimed): cyber-physical/AI."
    },
    {
     "front": "The S-curve of diffusion?",
     "back": "Slow start, explosive middle, saturation — adoption over time; infrastructure sets the slope."
    },
    {
     "front": "Christensen's Innovator's Dilemma (1997)?",
     "back": "Incumbents rationally optimize for best customers and are disrupted from below by cheaper, initially-worse tech."
    },
    {
     "front": "Kodak and the digital camera?",
     "back": "Kodak invented it (1975, Steve Sasson) yet lost — the dilemma is structural, not stupidity."
    },
    {
     "front": "The internet's architectural lesson?",
     "back": "Open protocols won the infrastructure layer; each layer enabled the next; distribution decides."
    }
   ],
   "quiz": [
    {
     "q": "The Second Industrial Revolution is associated with:",
     "choices": [
      "Steam and textiles",
      "Electricity, steel, chemicals",
      "The internet",
      "Bronze tools"
     ],
     "answer": 1,
     "explain": "~1870–1914: electrification, Bessemer steel, chemical industry, internal combustion."
    },
    {
     "q": "Moore's 'chasm' lies between:",
     "choices": [
      "Inventors and investors",
      "Early adopters and the early majority",
      "Hardware and software",
      "Startups and incumbents"
     ],
     "answer": 1,
     "explain": "Crossing from enthusiasts to the pragmatic majority is the go-to-market crux."
    },
    {
     "q": "A disruptive technology, in Christensen's sense, typically starts:",
     "choices": [
      "Better and more expensive",
      "Worse and cheaper, serving overlooked customers",
      "Identical to incumbents",
      "As a government project"
     ],
     "answer": 1,
     "explain": "Disruption enters low-end or new markets, then moves up as performance improves."
    },
    {
     "q": "TCP/IP standardization (1983) mattered because:",
     "choices": [
      "It was the fastest protocol",
      "It created a common open layer enabling the internet's growth",
      "It replaced telephones",
      "It was proprietary"
     ],
     "answer": 1,
     "explain": "An open, shared protocol layer let diverse networks interoperate — infrastructure for everything above."
    }
   ]
