  {
   "id": "m21-technical-writing",
   "code": "IL-21",
   "title": "Technical Writing",
   "weight": 1,
   "summary": "Clarity as a professional discipline: structure, precision, active voice, and documentation that people actually read.",
   "objectives": [
    "Structure a document for scanning, not just reading",
    "Write in active voice with concrete subjects",
    "Define terms before using them",
    "Produce documentation, RFCs, and status updates that get read"
   ],
   "notes": [
    {
     "title": "Write for scanning",
     "body": "Readers scan: put the **conclusion first** (BLUF — bottom line up front), then evidence. Use descriptive headings, short paragraphs, and lists for parallel items. A design doc's first paragraph should let a busy reader decide whether to read the rest — if it can't, rewrite it."
    },
    {
     "title": "Precision over decoration",
     "body": "**Active voice** with concrete subjects: 'The deploy script restarts the service' beats 'The service is restarted.' Define every term before first use; never let 'it', 'this', or 'thing' carry load. Numbers beat adjectives: 'p99 latency rose from 40ms to 900ms' beats 'latency got much worse.' Delete throat-clearing ('It is important to note that...')."
    },
    {
     "title": "The core documents",
     "body": "**RFC/design doc**: problem, goals/non-goals, proposal, alternatives, risks, rollout. **Runbook**: symptoms → diagnosis → fix, tested by someone who didn't write it. **Status update**: what changed, what's blocked, what's next — three bullets, no novel. **Commit message**: what and why, not just what (the diff shows the what)."
    },
    {
     "title": "Editing is the work",
     "body": "First drafts are for the writer; editing is for the reader. Cut 20% on the second pass as a discipline. Read aloud to catch tangles. The standard: a competent stranger, interrupted twice, can still follow it. Strunk & White's *Elements of Style* (1918) remains the compact classic — 'omit needless words.'"
    }
   ],
   "cards": [
    {
     "front": "BLUF?",
     "back": "Bottom Line Up Front — conclusion first, then evidence; write for scanners."
    },
    {
     "front": "Three rules of precise technical prose?",
     "back": "Active voice with concrete subjects; define terms before use; numbers over adjectives."
    },
    {
     "front": "Sections of an RFC/design doc?",
     "back": "Problem, goals/non-goals, proposal, alternatives considered, risks, rollout plan."
    },
    {
     "front": "What makes a good commit message?",
     "back": "What AND why — the diff already shows the what."
    },
    {
     "front": "Strunk & White's core maxim?",
     "back": "'Omit needless words.' (The Elements of Style, 1918.)"
    }
   ],
   "quiz": [
    {
     "q": "Which sentence is strongest?",
     "choices": [
      "The service is restarted by the script.",
      "The deploy script restarts the service.",
      "It is important to note the service gets restarted.",
      "Restarting of the service happens."
     ],
     "answer": 1,
     "explain": "Concrete subject, active verb, no throat-clearing."
    },
    {
     "q": "BLUF means:",
     "choices": [
      "Bold, large, underlined formatting",
      "Bottom line up front",
      "Brief, lucid, useful feedback",
      "Build, launch, update, fix"
     ],
     "answer": 1,
     "explain": "Lead with the conclusion so scanners can triage."
    },
    {
     "q": "A runbook is tested by:",
     "choices": [
      "Its author, carefully",
      "Someone who didn't write it, during calm",
      "The on-call during an incident",
      "Nobody — it's reference"
     ],
     "answer": 1,
     "explain": "Only a fresh reader proves the runbook is self-sufficient; never first-test it in a real incident."
    },
    {
     "q": "'Latency got much worse' should become:",
     "choices": [
      "'Latency degraded significantly'",
      "'p99 latency rose from 40ms to 900ms'",
      "'Users are unhappy'",
      "'We must fix latency'"
     ],
     "answer": 1,
     "explain": "Numbers beat adjectives — precise, checkable, actionable."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "m21-rhetoric",
   "code": "IL-21",
   "title": "Rhetoric & Persuasion",
   "weight": 1,
   "summary": "Ethos, pathos, logos; the Toulmin model; framing and audience analysis — the honest craft of moving people with reasons.",
   "objectives": [
    "Use ethos/pathos/logos deliberately and honestly",
    "Structure an argument with the Toulmin model",
    "Analyze an audience before persuading it",
    "Distinguish persuasion from manipulation"
   ],
   "notes": [
    {
     "title": "Ethos, pathos, logos (Aristotle)",
     "body": "Aristotle's *Rhetoric* (4th c. BCE) names three appeals: **ethos** (the speaker's credibility), **pathos** (the audience's emotions), **logos** (the argument's logic). Technical audiences over-invest in logos and starve the other two — but a correct argument from an untrusted speaker, framed without regard for stakes, still loses. All three are legitimate; the vice is substituting one for another."
    },
    {
     "title": "The Toulmin model",
     "body": "Stephen Toulmin (1958): arguments have **claims**, **grounds** (evidence), **warrants** (the inference license connecting grounds to claim), **backing** (support for the warrant), **qualifiers**, and **rebuttals**. In engineering reviews, fights are usually about *warrants* — 'load tests predict production' — not about the data. Name the warrant and the debate gets productive."
    },
    {
     "title": "Audience analysis and framing",
     "body": "Persuasion starts with the audience's **values, knowledge, and decision criteria** — not your material. **Framing** selects which aspects of a situation are salient: the same migration is 'risk reduction' to ops and 'feature velocity' to product. Honest framing foregrounds relevant aspects; dishonest framing hides them."
    },
    {
     "title": "Persuasion vs manipulation",
     "body": "The line: persuasion respects the audience's rational agency — reasons they could endorse on reflection. Manipulation bypasses it — hidden agendas, manufactured urgency, selective data. The test you'd apply to others' decks, apply to your own: would this argument survive the audience knowing everything I know?"
    }
   ],
   "cards": [
    {
     "front": "Aristotle's three appeals?",
     "back": "Ethos (credibility), pathos (emotion), logos (logic) — Rhetoric, 4th c. BCE."
    },
    {
     "front": "Toulmin model components?",
     "back": "Claim, grounds, warrant, backing, qualifier, rebuttal (1958)."
    },
    {
     "front": "In engineering debates, what do people usually fight about?",
     "back": "Warrants — the inference licenses ('load tests predict prod'), not the raw data."
    },
    {
     "front": "Persuasion vs manipulation: the test?",
     "back": "Does it respect rational agency? Would the argument survive full disclosure?"
    },
    {
     "front": "What is framing?",
     "back": "Selecting which aspects of a situation are salient for an audience — honest when it foregrounds relevant aspects."
    }
   ],
   "quiz": [
    {
     "q": "Citing your team's incident-free record to support a proposal appeals to:",
     "choices": [
      "Logos",
      "Pathos",
      "Ethos",
      "Kairos"
     ],
     "answer": 2,
     "explain": "Credibility of the speaker — ethos."
    },
    {
     "q": "In Toulmin's model, the warrant is:",
     "choices": [
      "The final claim",
      "The raw data",
      "The inference license from grounds to claim",
      "The rebuttal"
     ],
     "answer": 2,
     "explain": "The warrant is *why* the grounds support the claim — usually the contested part."
    },
    {
     "q": "Presenting only the metrics that favor your proposal is:",
     "choices": [
      "Good framing",
      "Manipulation by selective data",
      "Logos",
      "Ethos"
     ],
     "answer": 1,
     "explain": "Bypassing rational agency with selective evidence — manipulation, not persuasion."
    },
    {
     "q": "Before persuading, first analyze the audience's:",
     "choices": [
      "Org chart",
      "Values, knowledge, and decision criteria",
      "Calendar",
      "Budget"
     ],
     "answer": 1,
     "explain": "Persuasion starts from where the audience is, not from your slides."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "m21-economics-1",
   "code": "IL-21",
   "title": "Economics for Engineers I — Markets & Costs",
   "weight": 1,
   "summary": "Supply and demand, opportunity cost, marginal thinking, and cost structures — the economics inside every build-vs-buy and scale decision.",
   "objectives": [
    "Read a supply-demand diagram and predict price changes",
    "Apply opportunity cost and marginal analysis",
    "Distinguish fixed, variable, and sunk costs",
    "Evaluate build-vs-buy with total cost of ownership"
   ],
   "notes": [
    {
     "title": "Supply, demand, equilibrium",
     "body": "**Demand** slopes down (higher price → less wanted); **supply** slopes up. **Equilibrium** is where they cross — the market-clearing price and quantity. A demand shift (tastes, income) moves price *and* quantity; a supply shift (input costs, technology) does the same in the opposite direction. Price **ceilings** below equilibrium create shortages; **floors** above it create surpluses."
    },
    {
     "title": "Opportunity cost and marginal thinking",
     "body": "**Opportunity cost**: the value of the best forgone alternative — the true cost of any choice. **Marginal analysis**: decide at the margin — is the *next* unit's benefit worth its *next* unit's cost? 'We've already spent $2M' is irrelevant to whether the next $100K is worth it (see: sunk costs)."
    },
    {
     "title": "Cost structures",
     "body": "**Fixed** costs don't vary with output (the data center lease); **variable** costs do (per-request compute). **Sunk** costs are spent and unrecoverable — rational decisions ignore them. **Economies of scale**: average cost falls as output rises (high fixed, low marginal cost — the software business in one phrase)."
    },
    {
     "title": "Build vs buy, honestly",
     "body": "**Total cost of ownership**: license + integration + operations + the opportunity cost of the engineers building it instead of the product. Buying looks expensive and building looks free because salaries are already budgeted — that is the opportunity-cost illusion. Decide on TCO over the realistic lifetime, including maintenance you will definitely pay."
    }
   ],
   "cards": [
    {
     "front": "What shifts demand vs supply?",
     "back": "Demand: tastes, income, substitutes. Supply: input costs, technology, number of sellers."
    },
    {
     "front": "Opportunity cost?",
     "back": "The value of the best alternative you give up — the true cost of a choice."
    },
    {
     "front": "Why do rational decisions ignore sunk costs?",
     "back": "They're unrecoverable; only future costs and benefits should decide."
    },
    {
     "front": "Economies of scale in one phrase?",
     "back": "Average cost falls as output rises — high fixed, low marginal cost."
    },
    {
     "front": "The build-vs-buy illusion?",
     "back": "Building looks free because salaries are sunk-budgeted; TCO must include opportunity cost + maintenance."
    }
   ],
   "quiz": [
    {
     "q": "A price ceiling set below equilibrium causes:",
     "choices": [
      "A surplus",
      "A shortage",
      "No effect",
      "Higher quality"
     ],
     "answer": 1,
     "explain": "Quantity demanded exceeds quantity supplied at the capped price."
    },
    {
     "q": "$2M already spent on a failing project. The next phase costs $200K for $150K of value. Rational choice?",
     "choices": [
      "Continue — protect the $2M",
      "Stop — the $2M is sunk; marginal cost exceeds marginal benefit",
      "Continue — sunk cost proves commitment",
      "Ask for $2M more"
     ],
     "answer": 1,
     "explain": "Sunk costs are irrelevant; at the margin, $200K > $150K, so stop."
    },
    {
     "q": "Software has high fixed and near-zero marginal cost, so it exhibits:",
     "choices": [
      "Diseconomies of scale",
      "Strong economies of scale",
      "Constant returns",
      "No cost structure"
     ],
     "answer": 1,
     "explain": "Average cost collapses as users grow — the economics of the whole industry."
    },
    {
     "q": "A new GPU generation halves training cost. In supply-demand terms:",
     "choices": [
      "Demand shifts left",
      "Supply shifts right; price falls, quantity rises",
      "No change",
      "Equilibrium is abolished"
     ],
     "answer": 1,
     "explain": "Lower input cost shifts supply right — the standard supply-shock result."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "m21-economics-2",
   "code": "IL-21",
   "title": "Economics for Engineers II — Incentives & Games",
   "weight": 1,
   "summary": "Externalities, public goods, game theory basics, and mechanism design — why systems behave the way they do once humans are in the loop.",
   "objectives": [
    "Identify externalities and public-goods problems",
    "Find Nash equilibria in 2×2 games",
    "Explain the prisoner's dilemma and its escapes",
    "Design incentives instead of wishing for cooperation"
   ],
   "notes": [
    {
     "title": "Externalities and public goods",
     "body": "An **externality** is a cost or benefit imposed on third parties — pollution (negative), open-source software (positive). **Public goods** are non-excludable and non-rivalrous (clean air, basic research): everyone benefits, nobody wants to pay — the **free-rider problem**. Fixes: price the externality (taxes, cap-and-trade), assign property rights (Coase), or fund publicly."
    },
    {
     "title": "Game theory basics",
     "body": "A game: players, strategies, payoffs. A **Nash equilibrium** is a strategy profile where no player can *unilaterally* do better — stable, not necessarily good. Dominant strategies, best responses, and backward induction (in sequential games) are the working tools. Model the incentives before predicting the behavior."
    },
    {
     "title": "The prisoner's dilemma",
     "body": "Payoffs make mutual defection the Nash equilibrium even though mutual cooperation pays both more — the canonical model of arms races, price wars, and feature-copying. Escapes: **repetition** (tit-for-tat sustains cooperation), **contracts** with enforcement, **reputation** systems, and changing the payoffs themselves."
    },
    {
     "title": "Mechanism design: incentives as engineering",
     "body": "**Mechanism design** is reverse game theory: choose the rules so that self-interested play produces the outcome you want — auctions, congestion pricing, on-call rotations, bug bounties. The engineer's version: don't exhort people to cooperate; **design the payoff matrix** so cooperation is the equilibrium."
    }
   ],
   "cards": [
    {
     "front": "Externality? Public good? Free-rider problem?",
     "back": "Externality: third-party cost/benefit. Public good: non-excludable, non-rivalrous. Free-rider: benefit without paying."
    },
    {
     "front": "Nash equilibrium?",
     "back": "A profile where no player gains by unilaterally deviating — stable, not necessarily good."
    },
    {
     "front": "Prisoner's dilemma: equilibrium vs optimum?",
     "back": "Mutual defection is the Nash equilibrium; mutual cooperation pays more — the tragedy."
    },
    {
     "front": "Three escapes from the dilemma?",
     "back": "Repetition (tit-for-tat), enforceable contracts, reputation — or change the payoffs."
    },
    {
     "front": "Mechanism design in one line?",
     "back": "Choose the rules so self-interested play yields the outcome you want."
    }
   ],
   "quiz": [
    {
     "q": "A factory's smoke harms neighbors. This is a:",
     "choices": [
      "Public good",
      "Negative externality",
      "Nash equilibrium",
      "Sunk cost"
     ],
     "answer": 1,
     "explain": "A cost imposed on third parties outside the transaction."
    },
    {
     "q": "In the prisoner's dilemma, the Nash equilibrium is:",
     "choices": [
      "Mutual cooperation",
      "Mutual defection",
      "Alternating",
      "No equilibrium exists"
     ],
     "answer": 1,
     "explain": "Each player gains by defecting regardless of the other's move — defection dominates."
    },
    {
     "q": "Tit-for-tat sustains cooperation in repeated games because:",
     "choices": [
      "It is forgiving only",
      "Retaliation makes defection unprofitable over time",
      "It requires a referee",
      "It changes the payoffs"
     ],
     "answer": 1,
     "explain": "The shadow of future retaliation shifts the equilibrium toward cooperation."
    },
    {
     "q": "Congestion pricing on a highway is an example of:",
     "choices": [
      "A price ceiling",
      "Mechanism design: pricing the externality",
      "A public good",
      "Sunk cost"
     ],
     "answer": 1,
     "explain": "It charges drivers the congestion cost they impose — designing the payoff matrix."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "m21-law-policy",
   "code": "IL-21",
   "title": "Law & Policy Literacy",
   "weight": 1,
   "summary": "Intellectual property, liability, privacy regulation, and how policy gets made — what every technologist should know without pretending to be a lawyer.",
   "objectives": [
    "Distinguish copyright, patent, trademark, and trade secret",
    "Describe how software liability generally works",
    "Name the major privacy regimes and their logic",
    "Explain how technical standards become policy"
   ],
   "notes": [
    {
     "title": "Intellectual property, briefly",
     "body": "**Copyright**: protects expression (code, text), automatic on creation, long terms — open-source licenses (MIT, GPL) are copyright licenses granting permissions. **Patents**: protect inventions, require application and disclosure, ~20-year terms — software patentability varies by jurisdiction. **Trademarks**: protect brand identifiers. **Trade secrets**: protected by secrecy itself (Coca-Cola's formula). This is literacy, not legal advice — consult counsel for real decisions."
    },
    {
     "title": "Liability",
     "body": "Software has historically faced limited **liability** for defects — licenses disclaim warranties, and courts treat software as a service as much as a product. That settlement is under pressure: critical infrastructure, autonomous systems, and AI are pushing toward product-like liability. The direction matters for design: **document** safety reasoning as if a court will read it, because one might."
    },
    {
     "title": "Privacy regimes",
     "body": "The **GDPR** (EU, 2018): consent, data minimization, purpose limitation, the right to erasure, heavy fines — it reshaped global practice. **CCPA/CPRA** (California): opt-out of sale/sharing, deletion rights. Sectoral US laws cover health (HIPAA) and children (COPPA). The common logic: individuals get rights over their data; collectors get obligations. Design for data minimization from the start."
    },
    {
     "title": "How policy gets made",
     "body": "Policy moves through **legislation**, **regulation** (agencies writing rules under statutes), **standards** (IETF, W3C, NIST — often voluntary until referenced by law), and **litigation**. Technologists influence all four: writing standards, commenting on rulemakings, and — most powerfully — building the systems whose properties the law then assumes. Code is policy's raw material."
    }
   ],
   "cards": [
    {
     "front": "Copyright vs patent vs trademark vs trade secret?",
     "back": "Copyright: expression, automatic. Patent: inventions, applied for, ~20yr. Trademark: brand marks. Trade secret: protected by secrecy."
    },
    {
     "front": "GDPR's core principles?",
     "back": "Consent, data minimization, purpose limitation, erasure rights, accountability — EU, 2018."
    },
    {
     "front": "Why does software liability matter more now?",
     "back": "Critical infrastructure, autonomous systems, AI are pushing software toward product-like liability."
    },
    {
     "front": "Four channels of policymaking?",
     "back": "Legislation, agency regulation, technical standards, litigation."
    },
    {
     "front": "The engineer's policy move?",
     "back": "Design for data minimization; document safety reasoning; engage standards and rulemakings."
    }
   ],
   "quiz": [
    {
     "q": "An open-source license (e.g., MIT) is legally:",
     "choices": [
      "A patent grant",
      "A copyright license granting permissions",
      "A trademark",
      "Not legally meaningful"
     ],
     "answer": 1,
     "explain": "Copyright vests automatically; the license is the author's permission slip with conditions."
    },
    {
     "q": "GDPR's data minimization principle requires:",
     "choices": [
      "Collecting everything, just in case",
      "Collecting only data needed for the stated purpose",
      "Deleting all data yearly",
      "Storing data only in the EU"
     ],
     "answer": 1,
     "explain": "Purpose limitation + minimization: collect what the purpose needs, nothing more."
    },
    {
     "q": "Technical standards (IETF, NIST) become binding when:",
     "choices": [
      "Never",
      "Referenced by law, contract, or procurement",
      "Published",
      "They are old enough"
     ],
     "answer": 1,
     "explain": "Standards are voluntary until legislation, regulation, or contracts incorporate them."
    },
    {
     "q": "This module's stance on legal questions is:",
     "choices": [
      "It constitutes legal advice",
      "Literacy only — consult counsel for real decisions",
      "Law doesn't apply to software",
      "Ignore compliance"
     ],
     "answer": 1,
     "explain": "Stated throughout: this is orientation, not counsel."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "m21-synthesis",
   "code": "IL-21",
   "title": "Synthesis — The Technologist as Citizen",
   "weight": 1,
   "summary": "Bringing it together: how philosophy, history, writing, rhetoric, economics, and law combine in the judgment of a senior technologist.",
   "objectives": [
    "Integrate the humanities lenses on a single case",
    "Write a reasoned position on a technology policy question",
    "Argue a technical decision to a non-technical audience",
    "Maintain a personal learning practice in the humanities"
   ],
   "notes": [
    {
     "title": "One case, six lenses",
     "body": "Take deploying facial recognition for building access. **Ethics**: duties to subjects vs. aggregate safety. **Epistemology**: what do the accuracy claims actually establish, on what data? **History**: which past surveillance expansions are the precedent? **Economics**: who bears the costs, who captures the gains? **Law**: which regimes govern the biometric data? **Rhetoric**: how do you argue the decision to the people scanned? No single lens decides — judgment is the integration."
    },
    {
     "title": "The senior technologist's judgment",
     "body": "Senior judgment is not deeper technical knowledge — it is wider **context**: second-order effects, stakeholder legitimacy, reversibility, and the ability to say 'we should not build this' with reasons. The humanities are the training ground for exactly that: structured thinking about ends, not just means."
    },
    {
     "title": "Arguing to non-technical audiences",
     "body": "Translate without condescending: lead with **stakes** (what changes for them), then **options** with trade-offs, then your **recommendation** with reasons. Drop jargon or define it once. Expect values questions — 'is this fair?' — and answer them as values questions, not as technical puzzles."
    },
    {
     "title": "A personal practice",
     "body": "The course ends; the practice doesn't. Read one serious book a quarter outside your field. Write — memos, essays, reading notes — because writing is thinking. Argue with people who disagree with you, charitably. The goal is not to become a philosopher but to become a technologist whose judgment deserves trust."
    }
   ],
   "cards": [
    {
     "front": "Apply three humanities lenses to any deployment decision.",
     "back": "Ethics (duties vs outcomes), epistemology (what do the claims establish?), economics (who bears costs?), law (which regimes?), history (what precedents?), rhetoric (how to argue it?)."
    },
    {
     "front": "What distinguishes senior judgment?",
     "back": "Wider context: second-order effects, legitimacy, reversibility — thinking about ends, not just means."
    },
    {
     "front": "Structure for arguing to non-technical audiences?",
     "back": "Stakes first, then options with trade-offs, then recommendation with reasons; define jargon once."
    },
    {
     "front": "A sustainable humanities practice?",
     "back": "One serious book a quarter outside your field; write regularly; argue charitably with disagreeing minds."
    }
   ],
   "quiz": [
    {
     "q": "Judgment, as developed in this course, is best described as:",
     "choices": [
      "Deeper technical knowledge",
      "The integration of multiple lenses on ends as well as means",
      "Faster decision-making",
      "Avoiding all risk"
     ],
     "answer": 1,
     "explain": "Judgment = structured thinking about what is worth doing, using every lens."
    },
    {
     "q": "When asked 'is this system fair?', the skilled response:",
     "choices": [
      "Redirect to accuracy metrics",
      "Answer it as a values question with reasons",
      "Say fairness is subjective",
      "Cite the model's F1 score"
     ],
     "answer": 1,
     "explain": "Values questions deserve values reasoning — metrics alone dodge the question."
    },
    {
     "q": "The through-line connecting all six humanities areas is:",
     "choices": [
      "Memorizing dates",
      "Structured reasoning about human ends and means",
      "Anti-technology sentiment",
      "Literary criticism"
     ],
     "answer": 1,
     "explain": "Each area is a discipline of thinking about what matters and why — in service of better building."
    },
    {
     "q": "Which habit best sustains humanities learning post-course?",
     "choices": [
      "Re-reading these notes yearly",
      "Regular reading, writing, and charitable disagreement",
      "Avoiding philosophy",
      "Only reading technical docs"
     ],
     "answer": 1,
     "explain": "Practice, not review: read widely, write to think, argue well."
    }
   ]
  }
 ],
